  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge that has been in place over the Four Corners \nregion is still likely to stay nearly stationary over the next week. \nThroughout the week, Guidance does show some indication of \nshortwaves moving through the region. \n\nOverall, high temperatures look to \"cool\" down to the mid to high \n90s for the majority of the week. Winds for the week look to remain \ncalm to breezy, with relative humidity values in the 30-60% range. \n\nMoving to the precipation chances this week: starting with Tuesday, \nGuidance mainly the NAM and the RRFS shows a boundary over the \nsouthern part of the CO/KS border. With this boundary, moisture \nadvection from the south and will move northward of over the CWA. \nWinds will mainly be from the east, causing upslope flow and will \nhelp influence shower/storm production. PoPs are in the 30-50% range, \nand the probability of exceeding 0.10\" of precipitation is in the 15-\n30% for areas west of KS Hwy 27. However, one thing to note is that \nsome models do not show this boundary or show a weaker version on \nthe boundary. Given this is roughly four days out, it will be\ninteresting to see how Guidance will trend with the boundary and\nits location for precipitation chances.\n\nOnce again, Wednesday has similar precipitation chances with PoPs \nin the 20-50% range. Currently, Guidance looks to have another \nboundary in place, but there is a fair amount of disagreement on \nwhere the boundary will be located. Given we have moisture advection \nfrom the south, this will help the precipitation chances. \n\nLooking at the rest of the week, each afternoon/evening does \nhave around a 10-20% range for PoPs. These chances will be \ndependent on how Tuesday and Wednesday pan out. While a fair \namount can change, there is a signal that precipitation chances \ndo return to the area.",
